# BUILD BRIEF: [APP NAME] — No-Code Data Sync Platform (MVP v1)

You are a senior full-stack engineer. Build a production-ready MVP, not a mockup. Every screen must be backed by real data, real auth, real jobs, and real API calls. Work in the order given in Section 14, commit in small steps, and do not skip the acceptance criteria in Section 16.

## 0. ASSUMPTIONS (state these in the README, and follow them)
- App name is a placeholder. Read it from env var `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME` (default "SyncApp") so it can be renamed without code changes.
- Solo founder, small budget, so managed services everywhere and no self-hosted infrastructure.
- Initial ICP: freelancers and small agencies that run client work in Notion/Airtable and need leads or contacts synced with HubSpot or Google Sheets.
- Sync is record-level and eventually consistent. We do NOT promise sub-second latency. "Real-time" means webhook-triggered, usually within 1 minute.
- Each connector exposes "objects" (a Sheet tab, a Notion database, an Airtable table, a HubSpot object type, a CSV dataset). Each object has "fields" and "records".
- Currency is USD. English-only UI. Desktop-first, responsive down to tablet.
- Third-party API quirks (rate limits, scopes) must be verified against each vendor's current docs while building. Do not guess scopes or endpoints from memory.

## 1. PRODUCT SUMMARY
A web app where non-technical users connect two data sources, visually map fields, choose a sync direction, dry-run it, and activate it, all in under 10 minutes. The app then keeps records synchronized automatically, with a dashboard showing health, history, and errors.

Wedge: instead of "everything", we are the best, simplest way to keep Notion, Airtable, Google Sheets, HubSpot, and CSV data in sync, with trustworthy conflict handling and clear, human-readable errors.

## 2. V1 SCOPE

### In scope
1. Auth: email/password plus Google sign-in, email verification, password reset.
2. Workspaces (teams) with roles: Owner, Admin, Member, Viewer.
3. Connectors: Google Sheets, Notion, Airtable, HubSpot (Contacts, Companies, Deals), CSV upload/import (a one-time or re-uploadable dataset that acts as a source or destination).
4. Connected accounts via OAuth (Airtable/Notion/HubSpot/Google). Tokens encrypted at rest.
5. Visual sync builder wizard (Section 7).
6. Sync directions: one-way (A→B), one-way (B→A), two-way.
7. Sync triggers: manual, scheduled (interval), and webhook/real-time where the connector supports it, with polling fallback.
8. Operations: create, update, delete (hard delete OFF by default; soft-delete/archive or "ignore deletes" options).
9. Conflict rules for two-way: source-wins, destination-wins, newest-update-wins, manual review (review queue UI).
10. Field mapping with type detection, suggested mappings, required-field warnings, transformations.
11. Filters (per sync) on source records.
12. Dry-run/simulation before activation and on demand.
13. Sync dashboard, per-sync history, event log, error center with fix guidance, retries, dead-letter queue, manual replay.
14. Stripe billing: plans, 14-day trial, upgrade prompts, plan-limit enforcement, customer portal.
15. API keys (workspace-scoped) with a minimal REST API: list syncs, trigger a sync run, get run status.
16. Audit log, workspace data export, workspace deletion, connection deletion with data purge.
17. Marketing landing page + pricing page + docs stubs.

### OUT of scope for V1 (do NOT build)
- Salesforce, Shopify, Stripe-as-a-source, Jira, Trello, Postgres, or any connector beyond the five above. Only design the abstraction so they can be added later.
- Multi-hop pipelines (A→B→C), joins across sources, many-to-one merges, or aggregation.
- Code-based transformations, custom scripts, or formulas beyond the fixed transformation list.
- Attachment/file/image sync (skip file fields with a clear "unsupported" notice), Notion page body/blocks content (properties only), Airtable linked-record syncing beyond mapping to text (display names).
- On-prem agents, SSO/SAML, SOC2 tooling, white-labeling, mobile apps, AI features, a public marketplace, or a visual workflow canvas.
- Real-time collaboration or live cursors.
- Custom domains per workspace.
Where something is out of scope, show a clear "Not supported yet" message and never fail silently.

## 3. TECH STACK (use exactly this; do not substitute without a written reason)
- Framework: Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript (strict mode), deployed to Vercel.
- UI: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui + lucide-react icons. React Hook Form + Zod for forms and validation. TanStack Query for client data fetching.
- Database/Auth: Supabase (Postgres + Row Level Security + Supabase Auth). Use Drizzle ORM for typed schema and migrations.
- Background jobs: Inngest (or Trigger.dev if Inngest is unavailable) for durable queues, scheduled jobs, retries, concurrency limits, and step functions. The sync engine must be runnable as a job function so the same code can later move to a worker service.
- Billing: Stripe (Checkout, Customer Portal, webhooks).
- Email: Resend (transactional: verification, invites, failure alerts).
- Observability: Sentry for errors, structured JSON logs, and a `/api/health` endpoint.
- Encryption: AES-256-GCM envelope encryption using a `ENCRYPTION_KEY` env secret (support key versioning for rotation). Never log tokens.
- Testing: Vitest for unit tests, Playwright for 2–3 critical E2E flows, MSW or fixtures for mocking vendor APIs.

Why this stack (put a short version in the README): one language end-to-end, managed Postgres with RLS for tenant isolation, durable job infrastructure without running our own workers, fast to ship for a solo founder, easy to scale later.

## 4. ARCHITECTURE

### 4.1 Layers
1. Web/UI layer (Next.js).
2. API layer (Next.js route handlers/server actions), validating every input with Zod and checking workspace membership and role on every request.
3. Sync engine (pure TypeScript package in `/src/lib/sync-engine`), with no framework dependencies and heavily unit-tested.
4. Connector layer (`/src/lib/connectors/*`), one folder per connector implementing a shared interface.
5. Jobs layer (Inngest functions) that orchestrate engine runs.
6. Data layer (Drizzle schema + RLS policies).

### 4.2 Connector interface (TypeScript; every connector MUST implement it)
```
interface Connector {
  id: string;                       // "google_sheets" | "notion" | "airtable" | "hubspot" | "csv"
  displayName: string;
  capabilities: {
    read: boolean; write: boolean; delete: boolean;
    webhooks: boolean; incrementalSync: boolean; schemaDiscovery: boolean;
  };
  auth: {
    type: "oauth2" | "none";
    getAuthUrl(ctx): string;
    exchangeCode(ctx, code): Promise<TokenSet>;
    refresh(ctx, tokens): Promise<TokenSet>;
    requiredScopes: string[];       // least-privilege
  };
  listObjects(account): Promise<ObjectRef[]>;                 // sheets, databases, tables, object types
  getSchema(account, object): Promise<UniversalSchema>;       // fields + types + required flags
  readRecords(account, object, opts: {
    cursor?: string; since?: Date; filter?: Filter; pageSize: number;
  }): Promise<{ records: UniversalRecord[]; nextCursor?: string }>;
  createRecord(account, object, rec, idempotencyKey): Promise<WriteResult>;
  updateRecord(account, object, externalId, rec, idempotencyKey): Promise<WriteResult>;
  deleteRecord?(account, object, externalId, idempotencyKey): Promise<WriteResult>;
  registerWebhook?(account, object, callbackUrl): Promise<WebhookRegistration>;
  verifyWebhook?(req): Promise<WebhookEvent[]>;
  rateLimitPolicy: { requestsPerSecond: number; burst: number; respectRetryAfter: true };
  mapError(err: unknown): NormalizedError;                    // vendor error → our error taxonomy
}
```
Adding a new connector must require ONLY a new folder implementing this interface plus one registry entry. Document this in `/docs/ADDING_A_CONNECTOR.md`.

### 4.3 Universal internal data model
```
UniversalSchema { objectId, fields: UniversalField[] }
UniversalField { key, label, type, required, readOnly, options?, vendorType }
type = text | longtext | number | currency | boolean | date | datetime | email | phone | url | select | multiselect | user | json | unsupported

UniversalRecord {
  externalId: string,              // id in the vendor system
  values: Record<fieldKey, unknown>,
  updatedAt?: ISO string,          // vendor's last-modified timestamp when available
  deleted?: boolean,
  hash: string                     // stable hash of normalized values
}
```
Every connector normalizes vendor data into these types on read and denormalizes on write.

### 4.4 Sync engine algorithm (implement and document)
1. Load sync config, mappings, filters, and current state (including a `record_links` table mapping source externalId ↔ destination externalId).
2. Read changed records from each side: incremental by cursor/timestamp when supported, otherwise full read with hash comparison.
3. Apply filters. Normalize and transform per field mapping.
4. For each record, compute the action: create / update / delete / skip / conflict, by comparing against the last-synced hash stored in `record_links`.
5. For two-way syncs, a conflict exists when BOTH sides changed since the last successful sync. Resolve with the configured rule; manual review writes a `conflicts` row and pauses only that record.
6. Loop prevention: store the hash of the values we wrote; when reading back, ignore changes that match our own last write.
7. Write in batches within each connector's limits, using idempotency keys of the form `{syncId}:{runId}:{recordLinkId}:{action}` stored in `sync_events`, so a retried job never double-creates.
8. Record every outcome as a `sync_event`. Update run counters and `record_links`.
9. Respect plan limits (record count, frequency) before and during the run.

### 4.5 Reliability
- Per-connector rate limiting via token bucket; honor `Retry-After` headers; exponential backoff with jitter (e.g., 1s, 2s, 4s… capped at 5 min, max 6 attempts).
- Classify errors: `retryable` (429, 5xx, network), `auth` (expired/revoked token → mark connected account "needs reconnect", pause syncs, email owner), `mapping` (bad field/type/required missing), `permission`, `validation`, `unsupported`, `unknown`.
- Circuit breaker per connected account: after N consecutive failures, open the circuit for a cooldown period and mark syncs "paused (provider issue)".
- Dead-letter queue: records/events that exhaust retries go to `dead_letters` with payload, error, and attempt history. UI offers "Replay" (single or bulk) and "Dismiss".
- Webhook handlers must verify signatures where the vendor supports it, respond fast (<2s), enqueue work, and dedupe by event id.
- Concurrency: only one run per sync at a time (lock); skip or queue duplicate triggers.
- Token refresh handled centrally with a lock to avoid refresh races.

