Enforcement rules:
- Block activation of a sync that would exceed active-sync or connector limits, with an inline upgrade prompt.
- If record limit is reached mid-run, stop creating new records, finish updates, mark the run `partial`, and show an "Upgrade to sync the remaining N records" banner. Never delete data because of limits.
- Show usage meters (records, syncs, seats) in the sidebar and billing page; show a warning at 80% and a blocking prompt at 100%.
- Trial banner with days remaining; email at day 10 and day 13.
- Stripe: Checkout for upgrades, Customer Portal for management, webhook handler for `checkout.session.completed`, `customer.subscription.updated|deleted`, `invoice.payment_failed`. Webhooks must be idempotent and signature-verified. Payment failure → 7-day grace period with a banner, then downgrade to Free.

## 7. UI / UX REQUIREMENTS
Design: clean, modern, minimal SaaS. Neutral palette with one accent color, generous whitespace, 8px spacing grid, inter-style sans font, light and dark mode, accessible contrast (WCAG AA), keyboard navigable, with empty, loading, and error states on every screen.

### 7.1 Navigation
Sidebar: Dashboard, Syncs, Connections, Review Queue (badge count), Activity/Logs, Settings. Top bar: workspace switcher, user menu, upgrade button when applicable.

### 7.2 Onboarding wizard ("New Sync", also shown on first login)
Steps with a progress bar, autosaved as draft so users can leave and resume:
1. Connect source (choose connector → OAuth or CSV upload; reuse existing connections).
2. Connect destination.
3. Select data (pick object/table/sheet/database for each side; show a 5-row live preview).
4. Map fields (see 7.3).
5. Choose direction, conflict rule, trigger (manual/schedule/webhook), delete behavior, and filters (simple builder: field + operator + value, AND/OR groups, max 5 conditions).
6. Test: run a dry-run (see 7.4) and show a summary: would create X, update Y, skip Z, conflicts N, warnings.
7. Activate: confirm plan limits, then go live with an optional "run first sync now".
Goal: a user with two accounts ready can finish in under 10 minutes. Add inline help text for every step.

### 7.3 Field-mapping screen
- Two-column layout (source fields left, destination fields right) with drag-to-connect or dropdown selection.
- Auto-suggest mappings via name similarity (normalized, case-insensitive, synonyms like "e-mail"/"email", "tel"/"phone") plus type compatibility; show a confidence badge and a one-click "Accept all suggestions".
- Auto-detect and display field types with icons; warn on lossy or incompatible conversions (e.g., text→number) and offer a transformation.
- Required destination fields without a mapping are flagged in red and block progress unless a default value is set.
- Choose exactly one "unique key" field pair used for matching records across systems (suggest email/ID/name); warn if the key is not unique in a sample.
- Per-field transformations (fixed list only): date format conversion, text→number, number→currency formatting, lowercase, uppercase, trim whitespace, split full name (first/last), combine fields (concatenate with separator), default value if empty, map select values (value mapping table).
- Live preview of the first 5 records after transformations.

### 7.4 Dry-run / simulation mode
Executes the full engine with writes disabled. Produces a downloadable report (CSV) and an on-screen table of planned actions with before/after diffs, conflicts, warnings (e.g., "42 records missing required field Email"), and the predicted record count against plan limits. No data is written to external systems and no usage is counted. Dry-run is required once before first activation (can be skipped only with an explicit confirmation checkbox).

### 7.5 Dashboard
Cards: active syncs, syncs with errors, records processed (7 days), failure rate. Table of syncs: name, connectors (icons), direction, status, last run, next run, records processed, errors, actions (run now, pause/resume, edit, duplicate, delete). Filters and search. Trend chart of runs/records over time.

### 7.6 Sync detail page
Tabs: Overview, Mapping, Runs, Events/Logs, Conflicts, Settings. Runs table with status, duration, counts, trigger. Run detail view with per-record events, filterable by status/action, with before/after diff and a "replay failed" button.

### 7.7 Sync Health / Error Center
List of errors grouped by cause with plain-English explanations and a "Fix this" action that deep-links to the exact mapping or connection setting. Examples of required messages:
- "Your Airtable field 'Status' only accepts: Todo, Doing, Done. We received 'In progress' on 12 records. → Add a value mapping."
- "HubSpot needs 'Email' to be set for Contacts. 8 records have it empty. → Set a default value or filter them out."
- "Your Notion connection expired. → Reconnect Notion."
- "Google Sheets is rate-limiting us. We'll retry automatically in 2 minutes."
Include a dead-letter tab with replay/dismiss and bulk replay.

### 7.8 Review Queue (manual conflict resolution)
For records in conflict show both versions side by side with changed fields highlighted; actions: keep A, keep B, pick per field, apply to all similar. Resolved decisions are written in the next sync and logged in the audit log.

### 7.9 Connections page
List connected accounts with status, scopes, who connected it, and which syncs use it. Actions: reconnect, rename, disconnect (warn which syncs will pause; offer "disconnect and delete cached data").

### 7.10 Settings
- General (workspace name, slug, timezone), Team (invite/remove, change roles, pending invites), Billing (plan, usage, upgrade, invoices via Customer Portal), API Keys (create shows the key ONCE, stores only a hash; revoke), Notifications (email on failure/needs-reconnect/conflicts digest), Security (audit log viewer with filters), Data & Privacy (export workspace as ZIP of JSON/CSV; delete workspace with typed confirmation and 7-day soft-delete grace; delete a connection's data).

### 7.11 Marketing pages
Landing page (headline, 3 benefits, how-it-works in 3 steps, supported integrations grid, pricing table, FAQ, CTA), pricing page, privacy policy and terms placeholders, and a changelog stub.

## 8. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS
- OAuth wherever the vendor supports it; never store raw third-party passwords. Use PKCE/state parameters and validate redirect URIs.
- Encrypt all tokens/webhook secrets at rest with AES-256-GCM, key versioning supported; decrypt only inside server code; never send tokens to the client or logs.
- Request least-privilege scopes per connector (read-only scopes when a connection is only used as a source, expanding via re-consent when write is needed). List scopes in `/docs/SCOPES.md` with the reason for each.
- Row Level Security on all tenant tables, plus server-side role checks. Add tests proving workspace A cannot read workspace B data.
- Validate all inputs with Zod, parameterize all queries, escape output, set CSP/security headers, CSRF protection for state-changing routes, and rate-limit auth, API, and webhook endpoints.
- API keys: hashed storage (SHA-256 with prefix lookup), scoped to one workspace, rate limited.
- Redact PII and secrets from logs and error payloads. Store only the record data needed for diffs, and purge event payloads per plan retention.
- Audit-log security-relevant actions: login, invite, role change, connection added/removed, sync created/activated/deleted, API key created/revoked, export, deletion, billing changes.
- CSV uploads: size limit (10 MB / 50k rows in V1), MIME and extension checks, parse safely, protect against formula injection (prefix cells starting with `=`, `+`, `-`, `@` when exporting).

## 9. DATA DELETION AND EXPORT
- Workspace export: background job producing a ZIP (configs, mappings, runs summary, audit log, record links) emailed as a time-limited signed link.
- Workspace deletion: owner-only, typed confirmation, 7-day soft delete, then permanent purge job (including tokens, cached payloads, files).
- Connection deletion: revokes the token at the vendor where supported, deletes encrypted credentials, pauses dependent syncs.
- Never delete records in connected third-party systems unless the user explicitly selected `hard_delete` behavior (default `ignore`, with a confirmation warning).

